<article class="env-lemma" id="08Y0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08Y0">Lemma <span data-tag="08Y0">47.4.3</span>.</a> Let $(R, \mathfrak m, \kappa )$ be a local ring. Any finite $R$-module has a projective cover. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $M$ be a finite $R$-module. Let $r = \dim _\kappa (M/\mathfrak m M)$. Choose $x_1, \ldots , x_ r \in M$ mapping to a basis of $M/\mathfrak m M$. Consider the map $f : R^{\oplus r} \to M$. By Nakayama's lemma this is a surjection (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00DV" data-tag="00DV">10.20.1</a>). If $N \subset R^{\oplus r}$ is a proper submodule, then $N/\mathfrak m N \to \kappa ^{\oplus r}$ is not surjective (by Nakayama's lemma again) hence $N/\mathfrak m N \to M/\mathfrak m M$ is not surjective. Thus $f$ is an essential surjection. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>